      "rev_n_20_8d48cf_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_20_8d48cf_0",
        "node_id": "n_20_8d48cf",
        "thought_text": "Use a min-heap to store pairs of $(a_i, \\text{original index})$. Extract the minimum requirement, assign the current smallest valid distinct integer (starting from 1 or the previous assigned value + 1), and store the result. This avoids explicit sorting if the input is massive, though sorting is likely faster in Python for this constraint size.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8630512659326567407",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794590055.5222461
      },
      "rev_n_21_57b449_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_21_57b449_0",
        "node_id": "n_21_57b449",
        "thought_text": "Apply a greedy approach by processing users in arbitrary order but maintaining a set of used ratings. For each user, find the smallest integer $\\ge a_i$ that is not currently in the set of assigned ratings. While this is logically sound, it may be inefficient ($O(N^2)$ or $O(N \\log N)$ with complex data structures) compared to sorting, making it less optimal for the given constraints.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8630512659326567407",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794590055.522255
